d7 | 20-28\u00d7 | FCF yield 2.5% \u2014 among the lowest in industrials |\n| P/B | 9.32\u00d7 | 5-7\u00d7 | Implies >8.8% perpetual growth at 10% COE; achievable only in a world where EPS grows 8%+ forever |\n| PEG (EPS growth 12.2%) | ~3.4\u00d7 (based on fwd P/E) | \u22641.5\u00d7 | Pack figure 2.0 uses a different (likely 5-yr) growth rate \u2014 [Aggregator] |\n\n**3-5. Reverse DCF (what the market price implies)**\n\nAt $635, EV = $24.25B. Solving the 10-year DCF with my terminal assumptions (g=2.5%, WACC=8.5%):\n\n- **Required FCFF CAGR (years 1-10): ~12-13%**\n- At WACC 7.5%, required FCFF CAGR: **~10%**\n\nFor reference: CW's 12-year FCFF history (proxied by FCF) grew at approximately 6-7% CAGR. The market is pricing roughly **2\u00d7 the historical growth rate**, sustained for a decade, with no margin recession.\n\n**3-6. Sensitivity \u2014 intrinsic value per share**\n\n| WACC \\ g | 2.0% | 2.5% | 3.0% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 7.5% | $391 | $420 | $456 |\n| 8.5% | $291 | **$310** | $333 |\n| 9.5% | $224 | $239 | $256 |\n\n**Range: $224 \u2013 $456.** The entire 80% CI remains below the $635 market price.\n\n```\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```\n\n**3.5-1. Competitive response.** If CW keeps winning sole-source content on Virginia/Columbia, primes (HII, GD/EB) cannot easily switch suppliers mid-program \u2014 switching costs protect CW, and margins can keep expanding. Risk: primes start \"backward integrating\" into actuation/controls as their own backlog grows. Probability of a meaningful in-sourcing wave: moderate, slow-moving. Score: \u22121 (mild negative).\n\n**3.5-2. Reflexivity.** High stock price \u2192 SBC economically valuable (but CW SBC is small at 0.6% revenue); M&A currency strong; customer perception solid. Falling price \u2192 minimal risk to defense franchises given engineering-driven selection. Reflexivity is weak: CW's fundamentals are contract-driven, not market-feedback-driven. Score: 0.\n\n**3.5-3. Customer behavior evolution.** Defense demand is program-driven, not adoption-S-curve driven. As Navy submarine and AUKUS programs scale, CW moves from early-production to high-rate production \u2192 positive operating leverage, better absorption (visible in FY26 margin guidance). Commercial nuclear/SMR is early-adopter stage with 5-10 year revenue potential [Estimate]. LTV/CAC not relevant in this model.\n\n```\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n```\n\n**4-1/4-2. Scenario probabilities**\n\n| Scenario | Prob | Description | IV/share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Bear** | 30% | Defense budgets plateau post-conflict; program delays; revenue +4% CAGR; op margin 18.5%; WACC 9.0%; g=2.0% | **$228** |\n| **Base** | 45% | Revenue +7-8% (FY26-28) fading to +4-5%; margin expansion to 20% by FY29; WACC 8.5%; g=2.5% | **$310** |\n| **Bull** | 25% | AUKUS/Columbia/nuclear drive 8-10% growth for 5-7 years; margins 20.5-21%; WACC 7.75%; g=3.0% | **$459** |\n\n**4-3. Bayesian update.** Prior (Layer 1.5 base rate) of an industrial hitting 8%+ CAGR 5 years: ~35%. Updating factors:\n- Evidence FOR bull: record backlog $4.5B (1.16\u00d7 book-to-bill), Columbia-class budget +63% ($9.3B\u2192$15.2B), AUKUS $3B submarine industrial base funding, Q2 revenue beat, guidance raise. Likelihood ratio ~1.5\u00d7.\n- Evidence AGAINST bull: 10-year revenue CAGR was only 4.4%; ~49% of revenue is commercial (economic sensitivity); defense budgets are political. Likelihood ratio ~0.7\u00d7.\n- Posterior: bull scenario ~25-30%. I keep 25%.\n\n**4-4/4-5. Expected price & margin of safety**\n\n```\nExpected value = 0.30\u00d7$228 + 0.45\u00d7$310 + 0.25\u00d7$459 = $68.4 + $139.5 + $114.8 = $322.7\n50% CI: $290 \u2013 $380\n80% CI: $228 \u2013 $459\nMargin of safety at T0 price $635:\n  Base case: \u2212105%\n  Expected value: \u221297%\n  Bull case: \u221228% (!!!)\n```\n\n**Judgment: NONE.** The stock trades above even my bull-case intrinsic value.\n\n```\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550