ground vehicles, naval electronics), commercial aerospace (actuation, sensors, flight recording), and commercial nuclear power (pumps, valves, containment). It operates through three segments \u2014 Aerospace & Industrial, Defense Electronics, and Naval Power \u2014 with sole-source positions on many platforms. Revenue is ~$3.7B, growing 8\u201312%, with a backlog of $4.5B.\nUnderstandability: **High**.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat score**\n| Moat type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (IP, niche brand) | 2 | Stable \u2014 qualified defense IP |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Widening \u2014 sole-source programs, qualification barriers |\n| Network effects | 1 | Stable \u2014 limited direct network |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Stable \u2014 niche scale, restructuring benefits |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening \u2014 Chesapeake $80M expansion [Actual] |\n| Regulatory barriers | 3 | Widening \u2014 ITAR/defense procurement, nuclear certification |\n| Data moat | 2 | Widening \u2014 embedded computing, flight data franchises |\n**Total 15/21 \u2192 Wide moat**, direction: **Widening**. Evidence: record defense electronics orders (+50% YoY), backlog $4.5B (+10%), sole-source content on Columbia/Virginia submarines, F-35 and international fighters [Actual press release + transcript].\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch classification**: **Fast Grower** (revenue CAGR 3yr \u22489\u201311%, EPS growth 14\u201321%). Checkpoints: sustained double-digit EPS growth \u2713; low debt \u2713; healthy FCF conversion \u2713; tailwind from backlog \u2713; but P/E 48x and PEG 2.0 \u2192 growth is not cheap.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial strength scorecard**\n- Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR (FY22\u201325): (3.498/2.557)^(1/3)\u22121 = **11.0%** [Arithmetic]\n- Operating margin latest FY25: 18.1% vs 12-yr median 15.4% [Arithmetic]\n- EPS growth FY22\u201325: (12.87/7.52)^(1/3)\u22121 \u2248 19.6% [Arithmetic, using diluted EPS: 294M/38.7M=7.60, 484M/37.6M=12.87]\n- ROE: 19.7% [Aggregator];