l. leases \u2248 1.05x \u2014 still low.\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/data asset**: not applicable \u2014 defense/nuclear component maker, brand value not central.\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC**\n- GAAP NOPAT FY25: 634 \u00d7 (1\u22120.224) = $492M. Invested capital: equity 2,534 + debt 958 + op leases 210 = $3,702M \u2192 ROIC \u2248 **13.3%**.\n- Adjusted NOPAT (R&D capitalized): 492 + 105 \u2212 35\u00d70.776 \u2248 $570M; IC adj. + unamortized R&D (\u2248210) = $3,912M \u2192 adj ROIC \u2248 **14.6%**.\n- Gap 1.3pp \u2014 small; R&D not the dominant value-driver here.\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett)**\nOwner Earnings = NI 484 + D&A 125 \u2212 maintenance capex (~70 [Assumption]) \u2212 \u0394WC (\u2212141 = cash release) \u2248 484 + 125 \u2212 70 + 141 = **$680M** on a normalized cash-release year. Normalizing \u0394WC to zero: \u2248 **$539M**. I use FCF net of SBC in modeling \u2014 \u2248 $532M actual FY25.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\n## 3.1 Discount-rate derivation\n- Rf = 4.72% (10Y UST, Aug 21, 2026) [Actual \u2014 FRED]\n- ERP = 4.5% [Assumption]\n- Beta = 0.878 [Aggregator] \u2192 rounded to 0.90 [Assumption \u2014 pack beta is window-unspecified]\n- CoE = 4.72% + 0.90 \u00d7 4.5% = **8.77%**\n- Pre-tax cost of debt = 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 IG industrial, ~30bp over 10Y], tax rate 22.4% [Estimate \u2014 FY24 reported effective rate]\n- After-tax Kd = 3.88%\n- Weights: E = 23.451 / (23.451 + 0.958) = 96.1%; D = 3.9%\n- **WACC = 0.961 \u00d7 8.77% + 0.039 \u00d7 3.88% = 8.58% \u2192 use 8.6%**\n\n## 3.2 Explicit forecast\n| ($B) | FY25A | FY26E | FY27E | FY28E | FY29E | FY30E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 3.498 | 3.795 | 4.061 | 4.305 | 4.520 | 4.701 |\n| Growth | +12.1% | +8.5% | +7.0% | +6.0% | +5.0% | +4.0% |\n| EBIT margin | 18.1% | 19.2% | 19.5% | 19.8% | 20.0% | 20.0% |\n| EBIT | 0.634 | 0.729 | 0.792 | 0.852 | 0.904 | 0.940 |\n| NOPAT (tax 22.4%) | 0.492 | 0.566 | 0.615 | 0.662 | 0.703 | 0.731 |\n| D&A | 0.125 | 0.135 | 0.145 | 0.155 | 0.165 | 0.175 |\n| Capex | 0.090 | 0.117 | 0.135 | 0.145 | 0.155 | 0.165 |\n| \u0394WC (source = \u2212) | \u22120.141 | \u22120.020 | \u22120.015 | \u22120.010 | \u22120.005 | 0.000 |\n| SBC | 0.022 | 0.025 | 0.027 | 0.029 | 0.031 | 0.033 |\n| **FCF** | **0.554** | **0.595** | **0.613** | **0.653** | **0.687** | **0.708** |\n\n**Driver sourcing:**\n- FY26 revenue +8.5%: midpoint of management guidance 8\u20139% [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 press release]\n- FY26 EBIT margin 19.2%: guidance 19.1\u201319.3% [Actual]\n- FY26 capex $117M: transcript says ~+30% vs FY25 $90M [Actual \u2014 management statement]; capex/sales ~3.1%\n- FY26 FCF $595M: guidance $585\u2013605M [Actual]\n- FY27 growth 7%: **[Assumption]** supported by backlog $4.5B (1.26x revenue) and book-to-bill 1.16\u20131.2x [Actual]; above long-run GDP, below the 10%+ upper end some bulls use\n- FY28\u201330 growth 5%\u21924%: **[Assumption]** fade toward nominal GDP; NOT established by management guidance \u2014 management's 2024 Investor Day three-year targets were referenced in the FY25 release but specific figures are **[Unconfirmed]**\n- Margin path to 20.0% ceiling: **[Assumption]** based on restructuring, operating leverage, and management's \"top quartile margin\" language [Actual]; 20% is my assumed plateau, not a stated target\n- \u0394WC source of cash through FY28: transcript states target **<18% of sales** [Actual \u2014 management]; after that \u0394WC neutral [Assumption]\n\n## 3.3 DCF output\nWACC 8.6%, terminal growth 3.0% [Assumption = long-run nominal GDP; not the pack's growth rate, and >3% is a red flag].\nDiscount factors: 0.9208, 0.8479, 0.7808, 0.7190, 0.6621\n- PV(2026\u20132030 FCF): 548 + 520 + 510 + 494 + 469 = **$2,541M**\n- Terminal value = 708 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.086 \u2212 0.03) = $13,022M; PV = $8,622M\n- EV = $11,163M\n- Net debt: LT debt 758 + current portion 200 + finance leases 0 \u2212 cash 371 = **$587M** (op leases $210M excluded \u2192 conservative)\n- Equity value = $10,576M\n- **Per share = $286 \u2192 ~$290 base IV**\n\n**Sensitivity \u2014 terminal growth / WACC:**\n| IV/share | WACC 7.6% | WACC 8.6% | WACC 9.6% |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| g 2.5% | 350 | 271 | 218 |\n| g 3.0% | 382 | **290** | 230 |\n| g 3.5% | 428 | 314 | 245 |\n\n**Sensitivity \u2014 FY27-30 growth (at 8.6% WACC, g3%):**\n- 4% fade scenario: $250\n- Base 7\u21924% fade: $290\n- Sustained 8%: $355\n- Sustained 12% (market-implied): $470\n\n## 3.4 Multiple cross-check\n- Forward P/E (adjusted EPS $15.25 mid-guidance [Actual]): 634.99/15.25 = **41.6x** [Arithmetic]\n- EV/EBITDA FY26E: EV 24.04 / (729+135) = 24.04/0.864 = **27.8x** [Arithmetic]\n- P/FCF on guided FCF $595M: 23.451/0.595 = **39.4x** [Arithmetic]\n- Peer context: defense primes trade 22\u201325x forward [Estimate \u2014 HeyoTrade/defense outlook, retrieved 2026-08-15]; CW at 41.6x is a **70\u201390% premium** to primes.\n- PEG: 2.0 [Aggregator] \u2014 expensive on growth. Even on the bullish 12% EPS growth case, PEG is 3.5. Only at 20% EPS CAGR does PEG approach 2.0.\n\n## 3.5 Tornado \u2014 top 3 drivers\n1. **FY27\u201330 revenue growth** (range 4\u201312%): swing \u00b1$100/share\n2. **WACC** (7.6\u20139.6%): swing \u00b1$70/share\n3. **Terminal growth** (2.5\u20133.5%): swing \u00b1$55/share\nThese become the thesis KPIs below.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n**Step 3.5-1. Competitive response**: Sole-source positions make direct competitive attack difficult. Risk is program-level recompete or budget substitution (e.g., missiles vs submarines), not head-to-head competition. New entrants in nuclear components must qualify for decades \u2192 competitive moat stable. Impact: \u22121.\n\n**Step 3.5-2. Reflexivity**: Positive feedback exists \u2014 high stock price supports SBC-based talent attraction, acquisition currency, and customer confidence (long-duration defense credibility). Negative reflexivity is mild because CW has no reliance on equity issuance. Impact: +1.\n\n**Step 3.5-3. Customer behavior evolution**: Defense procurement is loyalty-driven; commercial nuclear is early-stage with AP1000/SMR adoption still being proven. If nuclear hits prototype stage (management says \"initial prototype stage\" [Actual]), an adoption S-curve could begin post-2028. This supports the bull case but remains unproven. Impact: +1 to bull scenario.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4. SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN)\n\n**Prior** (base rates, Layer 1.5): defense drawdown risk 30\u201340%; nuclear conversion 50\u201360%; quality-compounder multiple compression ~60% over 3\u20135 years.\n\n**Posterior scenario set** (updated by actual backlog, order trends, guidance raises):\n| Scenario | Prob | Description | FV/share |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Bull \u2014 Nuclear Renaissance | 20% | AP1000 order 2026 [Actual expectation], DOE financing up to 10 reactors by 2030 [Actual], defense budgets accelerate, revenue +10\u201312% through 2029, margins 21%, FCF grows 12% sustained, multiple holds 35x | $443 |\n| Base \u2014 Steady Compounder | 45% | Track record continues: 8%\u21924% growth fade, margins plateau 20%, FCF ~$600\u2013710M, WACC 8.6%, g3% | $290 |\n| Bear \u2014 Budget Cycle/Program Delay | 25% | Post-2027 defense budget compression, naval program timing slips, nuclear order slips, revenue +2% then 0\u20131%, margins fade to 17.5% | $200 |\n| Stress \u2014 Stagflation/Execution | 10% | Rare-earth supply duration, cost inflation, margin trough 16.5%, revenue stagnation, WACC 10% | $121 |\n\nProbabilities sum to 100%; Base is the single highest probability \u2713.\n\n**Step 4-4. Expected price** = 0.20\u00d7443 + 0.45\u00d7290 + 0.25\u00d7200 + 0.10\u00d7121 = 88.6 + 130.5 + 50.0 + 12.1 = **$281**\n- 50% CI \u2248 $200\u2013$360\n- 80% CI \u2248 $130\u2013$440\n- **MoS = (281 \u2212 635)/635 = \u221255.7% \u2192 None.**\n\n**Step 4-5. Bayesian note**: The last three guidance raises are the likelihood evidence; I have already incorporated them into FY26 revenue/margin. The **posterior is still far below the market price**, which embeds the bull scenario at ~100% probability rather than 20%.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4.5. TIME HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\n**Step 4.5-1/2.** Market implied horizon: reverse-DCF at price $635 shows the market requires ~10% FCF CAGR for 10 years at 7.5% WACC (or ~15% growth at 8.6% WACC) \u2014 i.e., the market is capitalizing 10 years of high growth plus a premium terminal. My horizon is 3\u20135 years (guidance visibility). Spread = 5\u20137 years of implied visibility beyond what management has actually guided \u2014 **arbitrage exists but against the market's optimism, not with it.** This is a short-side signal: the asymmetry at $635 favors underperformance over 3 years.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITIONING\n\n**Step 5-1. Conviction score (15 max)**\n| Factor | Score | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Business quality | 3/3 | Wide moat, Excellent financials, 12-yr F-score 8/9 |\n| Valuation attractiveness | 0/3 | \u221256% MoS; 41.6x fwd P/E; 27.8x EV/EBITDA |\n| Macro alignment | 2/3 | Defense/nuclear counter-cyclical tailwind vs rate drag |\n| Thesis clarity | 3/3 | KPI: backlog, margins, FCF conversion, nuclear order \u2014 all measurable |\n| Execution traceability | 3/3 | 3 consecutive guidance raises |\n| **Total** | **11/15 \u2014 High conviction (to the SELL side)** |\n\n**Step 5-2. Conviction decay**: \u22121 point per 6 months if no confirming evidence; here, current price action has already moved down 21% from 52-wk high \u2014 the short-side case is partially validated.\n\n**Step 5-3. Sizing**: At IV $281\u2013290 vs price $635 \u2192 **0% allocation**. No shares held; if held, reduce to zero or underweight to \u22641% (momentum risk only).\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n- Short interest: 2.2% of float, 3.18 DTC, MoM change \u221211.6% (shorts covering) [Aggregator]. Not a squeeze risk; shorts are not crowded. \n- Institutional ownership: 89.8% [Aggregator] \u2014 heavily institutionally held; ETF/passive flows dominate. Downside risk: index-driven selling if CW falls out of growth/momentum screens.\n- Insider activity: no cluster data [Unconfirmed]; insider ownership 0.37% \u2014 low but typical for mega-institution-held industrials.\n- Options/sentiment: not retrieved [Unconfirmed]. Analyst targets: low $700, mean $810.83, median $821 [Aggregator] \u2014 sell-side is 10\u201337% above price; consensus is strongly positive. This is a **contrarian negative signal** \u2014 at 41.6x forward, the market is already paying for perfection, and targets are another 28% above.\n- **Crowding risk score: 9/15** \u2014 high: 89.8% institutional ownership, defense/nuclear thematic crowding, sell-side targets far above price. Position size penalty: if held, cut 1\u20132pp.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\n- Correlation risk: CW is a defense/nuclear/industrial compounder with beta 0.878 [Aggregator]. It behaves as low-beta growth \u2014 positively correlated with defense ETFs (ITA), nuclear themes (SMR/OKLO), and high-quality industrials. \n- It offers: recession resistance (backlog), rate sensitivity (multiple risk), and thematic upside (nuclear).\n- In a portfolio already holding defense/nuclear exposure, CW adds no diversification. In a portfolio without defense, it is a **quality diversifier**, but only at a reasonable entry price (~$300\u2013350).\n- Net fit: poor at current price; good business, bad price.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6.5. KELLY SIZING\n\n- Edge = (Expected value \u2212 Price)/Price = (281 \u2212 635)/635 = **\u221255.7%**\n- Negative edge \u2192 Kelly fraction = 0%. \n- With the full bull-scenario weight (20% \u00d7 443 + 80% \u00d7 635 assumed no-move): no positive edge under any probability weighting that leaves bull below ~50%.\n- Breakeven bull probability needed for EV = $635: solve 0.20\u2192p: p\u00d7443 + (1\u2212p)\u00d7290 = 635 \u2192 p = (635\u2212290)/(443\u2212290) = 345/153 = 225% impossible \u2192 **Even a 100% bull scenario does not clear $635.** Only the very top of bull (+12% growth, 21% margins, 3.5% terminal, 7.6% WACC) approaches the price.\n- **Kelly = 0%.**\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 7. RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\n**Downside risks**\n1. Defense budget reallocation post-2027 (IFPC funding increase is in the FY27 budget, but overall budget caps remain) [Actual + base rate]\n2. Naval program timing: Columbia/Virginia schedule slips (Navy shipbuilding track record is poor)\n3. Nuclear order delay: AP1000 \"expected this year\" is expectation, not contract [Actual transcript]\n4. Rare-earth/supply-chain cost pressure [Actual transcript]\n5. Multiple compression: 41.6x forward P/E is 70\u201390% premium to peers; any growth miss \u2192 25\u201335% drawdown\n6. Q3 sequential margin decline on mix + R&D [Actual transcript]\n\n**Upside catalysts**\n1. AP1000 order \u2014 CEO explicitly expects it in 2026 [Actual]\n2. DOE long-lead financing for up to 10 reactors by 2030 [Actual]\n3. Defense Electronics orders +50% [Actual]\n4. Chesapeake capacity expansion \u2192 incremental nuclear capacity from 2027 [Actual]\n5. Further guidance raises (2 consecutive already in 2026)\n6. M&A (management says top capital priority) [Actual]\n\n**Monitoring KPIs (thesis integrity)**\n- Quarterly book-to-bill \u22651.0 (actual: 1.16 Q2-26)\n- Backlog growth \u226510% (actual: $4.5B, +10%)\n- Adjusted operating margin \u226519% (guided 19.1\u201319.3)\n- FCF conversion \u2265105% (guided 105%+)\n- AP1000/SMR order announcement \u2014 THE swing event\n- Q3-26 sequential margin: if OI down sequentially as guided, do not panic; if down >300bps, re-check\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 7.5. CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\n- Common bear triggers across portfolio: (1) all defense/nuclear longs share rate risk \u2014 10Y >5.5% would compress all high-multiple defense names; (2) defense budget resolution; (3) rare-earth/supply-chain inflation.\n- Tail correlation: in a 2022-style risk-off (rates + inflation), CW's low beta 0.878 provides some cushion, but at 41.6x forward P/E the drawdown potential is amplified.\n- CVaR (worst 5%): if defense budget cut + rates 6%, price could test 25x forward = $381 (\u221240%). CVaR \u2248 \u221235\u201340%.\n- Conclusion: CW adds tail-risk to a portfolio if held at this multiple \u2014 it is not a safe \"defensive\" at 41.6x.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 8. MONITORING RULES\n\nRe-evaluate immediately if:\n1. **AP1000 order does not materialize by Q4-2026** \u2192 cut bull probability from 20% to 10%, IV \u2212$25\n2. **Book-to-bill < 1.0 for 2 consecutive quarters** \u2192 demand inflection; bear probability \u2191\n3. **Adjusted OM < 18.5% in FY2026** \u2192 margin thesis broken\n4. **FCF conversion < 95% in FY2026** \u2192 working capital reversal\n5. **10Y yield > 5.5%** \u2192 WACC 9.6% scenario, IV \u2192 ~$230\n6. **Defense budget real cut > 5% in FY27/28 budget** \u2192 run Bear scenario\n7. **Buyback at > 40x forward EPS** \u2192 capital allocation downgrade (was at high-teens multiples historically)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 9. RED TEAM\n\n**Step 9-1. Short thesis (7 bullets)**\n1. At 41.6x forward adjusted EPS, CW prices in 12\u201315% FCF growth for a decade; its own FY26 guidance is 8\u20139% sales, 14\u201316% EPS, and no verified multi-year target beyond that\n2. Sell-side at $810 mean is 28% above market; consensus crowding in thematic defense/nuclear names creates drawdown risk if any single program slips\n3. Defense Electronics revenue was actually \u22123% in Q2-26 (order growth \u2260 revenue growth), showing lumpiness and timing risk\n4. Management explicitly guides Q3-26 sequential OI/margin DOWN (mix + R&D) \u2014 the margin expansion path is not linear\n5. Peers (primes) trade at 22\u201325x forward; CW's 70\u201390% premium to primes is unjustified given 5\u20137% revenue visibility beyond FY26\n6. Working capital \"improvement\" \u2014 \u0394WC was a \u2212$141M source in FY25 and \u2212$157M in FY24; this is not a permanent source: as backlog converts to inventory/receivables, WC could reverse and hit FCF\n7. If nuclear orders slip by 12\u201318 months (base rate for large infrastructure), the entire bull thesis shifts to a 8% grower at 41x \u2014 severe re-rating risk\n\n**Step 9-2. Logic combat: Long vs Short**\n- Long logic: exceptional quality, backlog 1.26x revenue, 1.16\u20131.2x book-to-bill, 3 consecutive guidance raises, nuclear renaissance option.\n- Short logic: price is 2.3x our base IV; market-implied growth impossible at base rates; multiple has no historical support; Q3 guidance already signals lumpiness.\n- Strength score: Long 6/10, Short 8/10. **Gap = 2 \u2192 conviction downgrade required.** (This supports AVOID, not aggressive short \u2014 shorting a 0.88-beta quality compounder is a crowded trade against the tape.)\n\n**Step 9-3. Adversarial data**: No Hindenburg-style report surfaced in my search window [Unconfirmed]. The Alphastreet article \"Curtiss-Wright: Nuclear Growth Priced to Perfection\" \u2014 access failed (403) \u2014 but its headline aligns with my conclusion [Unconfirmed content, cited as a source of concern].\n\n**Step 9-4. Killed arguments (and when they revive)**\n- \"Defense is recession-proof\" \u2014 Killed because defense budgets ARE subject to political reallocation; revives if FY27-28 NDAA shows real growth >5%\n- \"Nuclear renaissance is a sure thing\" \u2014 Killed because no AP1000 contract is yet signed; revives the moment an order is announced\n- \"Quality compounders deserve 40x\" \u2014 Killed because CW's own historical multiple range has been 15\u201325x; revives only if sustained 12%+ growth is demonstrated over 2+ years\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 10. AUDIT RESULTS\n\n- **A. Methodology**: Engine 1 chosen because CW is a cash-generative, wide-moat, steady grower; no transition symptoms (no FCF loss, no option-like value dependence). Engine 2 would misfit (defense is counter-cyclical); Engine 4 would overvalue. \u2713\n- **B. Valuation**: DCF explicit 5 years, terminal growth \u22643%, WACC derived from beta/Rf/ERP. \u2713\n- **C. Bias**: Risk of conservatism (I may be underestimating nuclear upside) is countered by bull scenario at 20%; risk of anchoring on sell-side is mitigated by showing source. \u2713\n- **D. Consistency**: Probabilities sum to 100; expected value computed; MoS negative; no single-scenario conclusion. \u2713\n- **E. Narrative**: \"Record backlog\" and \"record orders\" not taken at face value; order growth \u2192 revenue growth has a timing lag that is not guaranteed. \u2713\n- **F. Red team**: Section 9 complete; short thesis stronger than long at this price. \u2713\n- **G. Data integrity**: Pack continuity warnings addressed (no suspicious one-way steps in CW revenue). FY24-25 tax inputs not reconstructed. 2024 Investor Day targets [Unconfirmed] \u2014 flagged in forecast sourcing. FY25 full-year segment split not retrieved [Unconfirmed] \u2014 does not materially alter top-down DCF. \u2713\n- **Audit result: PASS** \u2014 no recalculation triggered, but all [Unconfirmed] items are disclosed above.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 11. DECISION JOURNAL ENTRY\n\n- **T0 date**: 2026-08-24; price $634.99; mkt cap $23.451B\n- **Base IV**: $290/share; expected value $281; 50% CI $200\u2013360; 80% CI $130\u2013440\n- **Key assumptions**: WACC 8.6%; FY27 growth 7% fading to 4%; margin ceiling 20%; terminal g 3.0%; tax 22.4%; FY26 guidance taken as management states\n- **Key uncertainties**: 2024 Investor Day 3-yr targets; AP1000 order timing; FY27-30 defense budget path; Q3-26 margin guidance vs full-year\n- **Red team concern**: Market may hold CW above IV for years on thematic flows; shorting the name is not recommended, only avoiding/pairing\n- **Decision**: AVOID at $634.99. If held: SELL (or reduce to \u22641% as a monitored momentum position with a hard stop at $550, i.e., 20% below T0; re-entry only \u2264$360 or on confirmed AP1000 order + backlog re-acceleration).\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 12. FINAL EXECUTION OPINION\n\n**Action: AVOID / SELL IF HELD**\n\n| Item | Recommendation |\n|---|---|\n| Action | Do not initiate; trim or exit existing positions |\n| Conviction | High (11/15) \u2014 direction: underweight |\n| Target weight | 0% |\n| Entry zone (if forced) | \u2264 $330 (base IV) with a hard discipline: 0.5x Kelly \u2192 3\u20134% max position |\n| Re-entry triggers | AP1000 order contract signed; FY27 guidance implying >8% growth; price \u2264 $350; 10Y < 4.0% |\n| Exit triggers (if held) | Any guidance cut; book-to-bill <1.0 for 2 quarters; price > $800 (sell into strength) |\n| Staging | None at current price. If re-entering at \u2264$330: 2 tranches, second tranche at \u2264$290 |\n\n**Bottom line**: Curtiss-Wright is a best-in-class quality compounder \u2014 wide moat, excellent financials, impeccable execution (3 consecutive guidance raises), and credible nuclear/defense tailwinds. But at $634.99, it trades at 41.6x forward adjusted EPS, 39x forward FCF, and 27.8x EV/EBITDA \u2014 a 70\u201390% premium to defense primes and 2.3x my base-case intrinsic value. My probability-weighted fair value is **$281/share** (range $130\u2013$440). The market is pricing in a perfect decade of 12\u201315% FCF growth and an immediate nuclear renaissance. Companies with 92 years of history should not be bought at prices that ignore 92 years of base rates. **No margin of safety exists. Pass.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nEND OF ANALYSIS \u2014 RS2 v2.0 | Data as of 2026-08-24\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550"}, {"sample": 3, "iv": 302.0, "plausible": true, "reasons": [], "flags": [], "truncated": false, "secs": 384, "report": "```markdown\n# RS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS \u2014 CURTISS-WRIGHT CORPORATION (NYSE: CW)\n\nT0: 2026-08-24 | Price: $634.99 | Market Cap: $23.451B | Shares: 36,931,100\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\n**Step 0-1. Enterprise Archetype: B \u2014 Quality Compounder**\n- 12-year operating history, revenue CAGR 7.9% (2019\u20132025) [Actual], ROIC \u224816% [Estimate], sole-source defense content, steady FCF conversion >100%.\n- Not Archetype C (Cyclical): defense backlog and program years smooth revenue; FY2020 revenue only -3.9% during the pandemic [Actual].\n- Not Archetype D/E: no dominant platform monetization or option-dominated valuation; embedded optionality is secondary.\n\n**Step 0-2. Valuation Engine: Engine 1 \u2014 Traditional DCF + Multiples**\n- Requires: stable margins, positive FCF, multi-decade program visibility, debt/EBITDA \u22481.0x [Actual].\n- Engine 2 (cycle-adjusted) would mis-state a backlog-driven company as commodity cyclical.\n- Engine 4 (expectation-driven) would over-weight unquantified nuclear optionality.\n\n**Step 0-3. Archetype Transition: None**\n- No qualifying transition; remains a Quality Compounder.\n\n**Step 0-4. Engine Selection Reasons**\n1. Revenue base grew from $2.24B (2014) to $3.50B (2025), a 4.1% CAGR with only two down years [Actual].\n2. Operating margin expanded from 12.6% (2014) to 18.1% (2025) without balance-sheet stress [Actual].\n3. Net debt/EBITDA \u22481.0x, interest coverage high, FCF conversion 116% TTM [Estimate].\n4. Alternative Engine 4 would require treating nuclear/SMR upside as a call option; data pack provides no segment-backed numerics for that.\n5. Alternative Engine 2 would undervalue a 10-year backlog franchise.\n\n**Model Confidence: Medium** \u2014 no segment profit data, no effective tax rate, no analyst Q&A; uncertainty explicitly carried into scenarios.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro Variables**\n| Variable | Level | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| UST 10Y | 4.71%, up 0.44pts YoY | [Actual] Trading Economics/FRED 2026-08-21 |\n| Fed funds target | 3.50%\u20133.75%, held 9\u20133 vote | [Actual] FOMC 2026-07-29 |\n| DXY | ~98.6, down ~0.9% on 8/19 | [Actual] Trading Economics 2026-08-20 |\n| FOMC projected path | End-2026 3.8%, 2027 3.6%, 2028 3.4% | [Estimate] FundedFast/FRED median dots |\n| CPI / inflation | Not retrieved | [Unconfirmed] |\n| Credit spreads | Not retrieved | [Unconfirmed] |\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime Probability Distribution**\n| Regime | Probability | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 40% | 10Y at 4.7%, defense/industrial growth resilient |\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 25% | Fed pivoting to cuts in 2027\u201328 |\n| Stagflation (high rates + low growth) | 20% | Elevated rates with geopolitical supply risk |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 15% | Cycle late-stage risk; defense is counter-cyclical |\n\n**Step 1-3. Sensitivity Axes (-3 to +3)**\n- Interest rate sensitivity: **-1** (long-duration EPS but low leverage)\n- Dollar sensitivity: **-2** (weak dollar supports international defense/commercial; some imported-cost risk)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: **-1** (equity-linked buyback but no balance-sheet dependence)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: **-1** (defense counter-cyclical; commercial partial offset)\n- Commodity sensitivity: **0**\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro Alignment: Headwind (-0.5 conviction)**\n- 10Y at 4.71% pressures the 40x+ multiple; DXY weakness is a modest tailwind; defense budget growth is structural.\n- Weighted macro impact: mild negative for a long at current valuation.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base Rates**\n- Quality compounders with wide moats, ROIC > WACC and long-cycle contracts: 5-year survival \u224890%+ [Base rate].\n- Sole-source defense-qualified suppliers: revenue persistence probability high, 80\u201390% [Base rate].\n- Large defense program delays: 30\u201340% probability of a major schedule slip over a decade [Base rate].\n- Platform/nuclear optionality success: ~20\u201330% [Base rate].\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate**\nThe bull case embeds 8\u20139% revenue growth through 2027 and 20%+ terminal margins. Justification:\n1. Record backlog $4.5B [Actual] and book-to-bill 1.16x [Actual] provide visibility to 2027.\n2. FY2026 guidance raised twice; Q2 EPS +15% adjusted [Actual].\n3. FY2027 US naval shipbuilding request funds 4th Columbia-class and two Virginia-class [Estimate, FY2027 DoD budget press].\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check**\n- Bull scenario implied success probability (~30% to $520) is not >1.5x the applicable base rate; no red flag.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n**Step 2-1. Business Understanding**\nCurtiss-Wright supplies engineered components and subsystems for submarines, fighter jets, naval power, commercial aerospace, and commercial/industrial markets. It earns recurring aftermarket and sole-source revenue across decades-long defense programs. Understandability: **High**.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat Assessment**\n| Moat | Score /3 |\n|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (sole-source, qualified designs) | 3 |\n| Switching costs (program re-qualification) | 3 |\n| Network effects | 0 |\n| Cost advantage | 2 |\n| Economies of scale | 2 |\n| Regulatory barriers (ITAR, nuclear certification) | 3 |\n| Data moat | 1 |\n| **Total** | **14/21 = Wide moat** |\n\nMoat Direction: **Widening** \u2014 naval programs expanding, fighter content solid, commercial nuclear prototype stage, record defense-electronics orders [Actual].\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch Classification: Stalwart with Fast-Grower characteristics**\n- Revenue growth 11.0% 3-yr CAGR [Actual], EPS growth mid-teens.\n- Key Lynch check: can it sustain 10\u201315% EPS growth? Yes, via backlog and margins; risk is multiple, not business.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard**\n| Metric | Value | Tag |\n|---|---|---|\n| Revenue 3-yr CAGR | 11.0% | [Actual] |\n| Operating margin FY25 / TTM | 18.1% / ~19.4% | [Actual] |\n| ROE (vendor) | 19.7% | [Aggregator] |\n| ROIC (approx) | ~16% | [Estimate] |\n| FCF margin TTM | 17.1% | [Estimate] |\n| FCF conversion TTM | 116% | [Actual/Arithmetic] |\n| Net debt/EBITDA (LT+current debt only) | ~1.3x | [Estimate] |\n| Net debt/EBITDA (incl. op leases) | ~1.7x | [Estimate] |\n| Piotroski F-Score | 8/9 | [Actual/Arithmetic] |\n| Accruals ratio | -0.031 | [Actual/Arithmetic] |\n| Verdict: **Excellent** | | |\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (/15)**\n| Item | Score |\n|---|---|\n| Buyback efficiency (shares -2.4%/yr 3-yr, no peak excess) | 2 |\n| M&A track record (no data in pack) | 1* |\n| Capex efficiency (incremental ROIC >20% implied) | 3 |\n| Mix rationality | 2 |\n| Guidance accuracy (raises; multi-quarter beats) | 3 |\n| **Subtotal** | 11 |\n| Insider ownership 0.37%: no bonus | 0 |\n| Insider net buy/sell: no data | 0 |\n| **Total** | **11/15 \u2013 Good** |\n\n\\* M&A data unavailable; score set at 1 as conservative.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n- R&D data not in pack: no capitalization adjustment performed [Unconfirmed].\n- SBC adjustment: FY25 SBC $0.022B [Actual]; TTM adjusted FCF = $0.625B - ~$0.022B \u2248 $0.603B [Estimate].\n- Operating lease capitalization: FY25 operating leases $0.210B [Actual]; if treated as debt, total debt = $0.758B LT + $0.200B current portion + $0.210B operating leases = $1.168B, matching vendor total debt [Actual/Arithmetic].\n- Adjusted Net Debt: $0.958B \u2013 $0.371B cash = $0.587B (excl. leases); $0.797B (incl. leases).\n- Owner Earnings (FY25): NI $0.484B + D&A $0.125B - maintenance capex ~$0.045B - \u0394WC (-$0.141B) \u2248 $0.705B [Estimate].\n- Adjusted ROIC > GAAP ROIC because SBC is small and R&D is expensed; gap <5pp, no material distortion.\n\n---\n\n## SECTION 3. VALUATION \u2014 ENGINE 1\n\n### 3-A. Discount Rate Derivation\n\n| Input | Value | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| Risk-free rate | 4.71% | [Actual] UST 10Y 8/21/2026 |\n| Equity risk premium | 5.0% | [Assumption] |\n| Beta | 0.878 | [Aggregator] vendor, window unverified |\n| Cost of equity | 4.71% + 0.878\u00d75.0% = 9.10% | [Estimate] |\n| Pre-tax cost of debt | 5.0% | [Assumption] |\n| After-tax cost of debt | 3.95% | [Estimate] |\n| Debt used | LT $0.758B + current $0.200B = $0.958B | [Actual] |\n| Equity value | $23.451B | [Actual/Aggregator] |\n| WACC | 96.1%\u00d79.10% + 3.9%\u00d73.95% = **8.9%** | [Estimate] |\n\n### 3-B. Explicit Forecast (Base Case, $B)\n\n| FY | Revenue | Op Margin | Op Income | NOPAT | D&A | Capex | \u0394WC | UFCF |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2026 | $3.795 | 19.2% | $0.729 | $0.576 | $0.133 | $0.114 | $0.008 | $0.587 |\n| 2027 | $4.098 | 19.5% | $0.799 | $0.631 | $0.143 | $0.123 | $0.008 | $0.643 |\n| 2028 | $4.385 | 19.7% | $0.864 | $0.683 | $0.153 | $0.132 | $0.009 | $0.695 |\n| 2029 | $4.648 | 19.9% | $0.925 | $0.731 | $0.163 | $0.139 | $0.009 | $0.746 |\n| 2030 | $4.881 | 20.0% | $0.976 | $0.771 | $0.171 | $0.146 | $0.010 | $0.786 |\n| 2031 | $5.076 | 20.0% | $1.015 | $0.802 | $0.178 | $0.152 | $0.010 | $0.818 |\n| 2032 | $5.253 | 20.0% | $1.051 | $0.830 | $0.184 | $0.158 | $0.011 | $0.845 |\n| 2033 | $5.411 | 20.0% | $1.082 | $0.855 | $0.189 | $0.162 | $0.011 | $0.871 |\n| 2034 | $5.573 | 20.0% | $1.115 | $0.881 | $0.195 | $0.167 | $0.011 | $0.898 |\n| 2035 | $5.740 | 20.0% | $1.148 | $0.907 | $0.201 | $0.172 | $0.011 | $0.925 |\n\n**Forecast Driver Sources:**\n- 2026 revenue: management guidance 8\u20139% growth, midpoint 8.5% [Actual, company press release 8/5/2026].\n- 2027 growth: +8.0% [Assumption] (consensus forward revenue growth 8.24% [Estimate, yfinance]; backlog visibility supports).\n- 2028\u20132030 growth: 7%\u21926%\u21925% [Assumption] \u2014 no data beyond 2027; faded deliberately.\n- 2031\u20132035 growth: 4%\u21923.5%\u21923% [Assumption] \u2014 terminal fade.\n- Operating margin 2026: 19.1\u201319.3% guidance midpoint [Actual], 19.2%.\n- Operating margin 2027+: expansion to 20.0% by 2030 [Assumption] \u2014 credible from mix but unverified.\n- D&A 3.5% of revenue [Assumption] \u2014 FY25 D&A/revenue = 3.6% [Actual].\n- Capex 3.0% of revenue [Assumption] \u2014 FY25 capex/revenue = 2.6% [Actual]; guide implies higher 2026 capex.\n- \u0394WC 0.2% of revenue [Assumption] \u2014 FY25 working capital ratio = 17.5% of revenue; 0.2% is conservative relative to actual swings [Actual/Arithmetic].\n\n### 3-C. DCF Result\n- PV of explicit UFCFs: **$4.869B**\n- Terminal value = $0.925 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.089 \u2013 0.03) = **$16.15B**\n- PV of terminal value: **$6.883B**\n- Enterprise value: **$11.752B**\n- Cash: +$0.371B [Actual]\n- Debt: \u2212$0.958B [Actual, LT + current portion]\n- Equity value: **$11.165B**\n- Per share: **$302**\n\n### 3-D. Multiple Cross-Check\n- 2027E EPS: 2026 guide $15.25 midpoint [Actual] \u00d7 (1+11.7% consensus growth [Estimate, Zacks/TradingView]) = **$17.03**.\n- At current $634.99, forward P/E is **37.3x**.\n- Normalized 20\u201325x forward P/E on $17.03 = **$341\u2013$426**.\n- Current trailing P/E 48.1x [Aggregator] vs peers ~26x [Estimate, fullratio.com].\n- DCF and multiple methods both point to fair value **$300\u2013$425**, far below market.\n\n### 3-E. Tornado Sensitivity (Base IV $302)\n| Variable | -1\u03c3 / downside | +1\u03c3 / upside |\n|---|---|---|\n| WACC 8.9% \u00b1 1.0% | $241 | $376 |\n| 2035 terminal FCF \u00b1 10% | $275 | $330 |\n| Terminal growth 3.0% \u00b1 0.5% | $275 | $330 |\n| 2027 revenue +8% vs +6% | $298 | $306 |\n\n**Top-3 Thesis KPIs: WACC/rates, terminal margin, te