: [], "truncated": false, "secs": 338, "report": "Understood. Task budget exhausted \u2014 I have the necessary material from the pack plus retrieved primary sources (Curtiss-Wright Q2 2026 press release, Q2 2026 Fool transcript, FY2025 press release, FRED 10-year yield). Missing items (Fed stance specifics, DXY, credit spreads, exact 2024 Investor Day targets, FY25 segment split) will be marked [Unconfirmed] and carried through my scenarios.\n\nI will now write the complete report.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 RS2 v2.0 INTEGRATED INVESTMENT ANALYSIS \u2014 CURTISS-WRIGHT CORP (CW)\n\u25a0 T0: 2026-08-24 | FYE: 2025-12-31 | Price: $634.99 [Aggregator]\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\n# SECTION 0. ANALYSIS OVERVIEW\n\n- **Ticker**: CW, Industrials / Aerospace & Defense\n- **T0 price**: $634.99, mkt cap $23.451B, EV \u2248 $24.04B (net debt $587M incl. current portion of LT debt)\n- **T-1 reference**: FY2025 (actual): revenue $3.498B, OI $634M, NI $484M, OCF $643M, FCF $554M\n- **Tpf**: Q2 2026 report (filed 2026-08-06): sales $924M +5%, adj op margin 19.4%, adj diluted EPS $3.72 +15%, FCF $160M, backlog $4.5B; guidance RAISED for FY26\n- **Model type**: Engine 1 (Quality Compounder: DCF + Multiples)\n- **Computed intrinsic value (base)**: $290/share \u2014 80% CI $130\u2013$440\n- **Expected value (prob-weighted)**: ~$281/share\n- **Margin of safety at $634.99**: \u221255% \u2192 **No margin of safety**\n- **Final action**: AVOID / SELL if held. 0% target weight. Do not initiate.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1. MACRO CONTEXT\n\n**Step 1-1. Macro variable check**\n- 10-Year UST: **4.72%** (FRED H.15, 2026-08-21) [Actual]\n- Real rate: ~1.9\u20132.0% at 2.7% core inflation [Estimate]\n- DXY: not retrieved \u2014 [Unconfirmed]\n- Fed stance: not explicitly retrieved \u2014 [Unconfirmed]; 10Y at 4.7% and no easing signal in retrieved material suggests neutral-to-tight\n- Global liquidity: [Unconfirmed]\n- Economic cycle: late expansion / early deceleration, GDP growth positive but defense budget and nuclear investment act counter-cyclically\n- Inflation: core ~2.7% [Estimate]; CW management flagged rare-earth/supply-chain cost pressure [Actual transcript]\n- Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not retrieved\n\n**Step 1-2. Regime classification**\n| Regime | Probability |\n|---|---|\n| Goldilocks (low rates + growth) | 15% |\n| Reflation (high rates + growth) | 50% |\n| Stagflation (high rates + low growth) | 25% |\n| Recession (low rates + low growth) | 10% |\nProbabilities sum to 100%. [Assumption \u2014 based on 4.7% 10Y, positive but moderating growth, inflation elevated]\n\n**Step 1-3. Sensitivity matrix (each \u22123 to +3, positive = hurts)**\n- Interest rate sensitivity: +2 (defense revenue is long-duration/backlog; but rate levels drive multiple compression)\n- Dollar sensitivity: +1 (some international, but majority US defense dollars)\n- Liquidity sensitivity: \u22121 (low cyclicality; large-cap quality bid)\n- Economic cycle sensitivity: \u22121 (defense counter-cyclical; commercial aero/industrial mildly cyclical)\n- Commodity sensitivity: \u22122 (raw material cost pass-through, some rare-earth exposure)\nNet sensitivity: \u22121 (slightly defensive).\n\n**Step 1-4. Macro alignment**: **Neutral-to-slight-tailwind**. Defense + nuclear are secular, budget-driven; rate level is the main drag on multiple. Conviction adjustment: +0 (neutral).\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1.5. BASE RATE CHECK\n\n**Step 1.5-1. Base rates [Estimate]**\n- 5-year survival for an established S&P 400/600 industrials compounder with 12 years of filed history and $3.5B revenue: **~92\u201395%**\n- Probability a wide-moat, sole-source defense supplier grows FCF through a normal defense cycle: **~70%**\n- Probability of defense budget drawdown (real-terms cut >10%) within any 10-year window: **~30\u201340%**\n- Probability a large program (AP1000/SMR) converts into material revenue within 5 years: **~50\u201360%** [Assumption]\n- Probability a quality compounder's multiples compress from >40x toward 25x over 3\u20135 years: **~60%** [Assumption \u2014 mean reversion base rate]\n\n**Step 1.5-2. Deviation from base rate.**\nMy 5-year 4\u20138% FCF growth path is **below** the market-implied 12%+ FCF CAGR. Company-specific justifications for below-consensus growth:\n1. Defense budgets grow 2\u20134% real, not 12%; CW grows faster via share and program ramp, but 12% FCF growth for five years requires simultaneous submarine ramp + nuclear renaissance + commercial aero upcycle \u2014 all three at once is beyond base rate\n2. Guidance only extends to FY26 (sales +8\u20139%); no verified multi-year target for FY27\u2013FY30 retrieved (2024 Investor Day figures [Unconfirmed])\n3. Management itself guided Q3 OI/margin **down sequentially** on mix and R&D [Actual transcript]\nDeviation justified: I am within base rate, market is 2x above base rate \u2192 base rate check flags the **market's** expectation as aggressive.\n\n**Step 1.5-3. Base-rate shock**: Market-implied success (perpetual \u226512% FCF growth) is >2x my base-rate-supported case \u2192 Red Flag against the long side, not against my analysis.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\n**Step 2-1. Understanding (3 sentences)**\nCurtiss-Wright makes mission-critical, highly engineered components and subsystems for defense platforms (submarines, fighter jets, ground vehicles, naval electronics), commercial aerospace (actuation, sensors, flight recording), and commercial nuclear power (pumps, valves, containment). It operates through three segments \u2014 Aerospace & Industrial, Defense Electronics, and Naval Power \u2014 with sole-source positions on many platforms. Revenue is ~$3.7B, growing 8\u201312%, with a backlog of $4.5B.\nUnderstandability: **High**.\n\n**Step 2-2. Moat score**\n| Moat type | Score (0\u20133) | Direction |\n|---|---|---|\n| Intangible assets (IP, niche brand) | 2 | Stable \u2014 qualified defense IP |\n| Switching costs | 3 | Widening \u2014 sole-source programs, qualification barriers |\n| Network effects | 1 | Stable \u2014 limited direct network |\n| Cost advantage | 2 | Stable \u2014 niche scale, restructuring benefits |\n| Economies of scale | 2 | Widening \u2014 Chesapeake $80M expansion [Actual] |\n| Regulatory barriers | 3 | Widening \u2014 ITAR/defense procurement, nuclear certification |\n| Data moat | 2 | Widening \u2014 embedded computing, flight data franchises |\n**Total 15/21 \u2192 Wide moat**, direction: **Widening**. Evidence: record defense electronics orders (+50% YoY), backlog $4.5B (+10%), sole-source content on Columbia/Virginia submarines, F-35 and international fighters [Actual press release + transcript].\n\n**Step 2-3. Lynch classification**: **Fast Grower** (revenue CAGR 3yr \u22489\u201311%, EPS growth 14\u201321%). Checkpoints: sustained double-digit EPS growth \u2713; low debt \u2713; healthy FCF conversion \u2713; tailwind from backlog \u2713; but P/E 48x and PEG 2.0 \u2192 growth is not cheap.\n\n**Step 2-4. Financial strength scorecard**\n- Revenue growth 3-yr CAGR (FY22\u201325): (3.498/2.557)^(1/3)\u22121 = **11.0%** [Arithmetic]\n- Operating margin latest FY25: 18.1% vs 12-yr median 15.4% [Arithmetic]\n- EPS growth FY22\u201325: (12.87/7.52)^(1/3)\u22121 \u2248 19.6% [Arithmetic, using diluted EPS: 294M/38.7M=7.60, 484M/37.6M=12.87]\n- ROE: 19.7% [Aggregator]; ROIC: ~19% [Estimate]\n- FCF margin: 554/3,498 = 15.8%; FCF conversion vs OCF = 554/643 = 86%; OCF/NI = 133% [Arithmetic]\n- Net debt/EBITDA: (958\u2212371)/759 = **0.77x** [Arithmetic]; interest coverage >15x [Estimate]\n- Net debt structure: low leverage, $587M net debt, plus $210M operating leases [filed]\n**Judgment: Excellent.**\n\n**Step 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard** (0\u201315)\n1. Buyback efficiency: $465M bought in 2025; $260M planned 2026; share count \u22121.9%/yr, buybacks done at high-teens EPS, not at peak bubble multiples \u2192 **3/3**\n2. M&A track record: I/C Solutions acquisition contributing [Actual FY25 Q4 release], but multi-year ROIC on M&A [Unconfirmed] \u2192 **2/3**\n3. Capex efficiency: Incremental ROIC high; capex/sales only ~2.6%; Chesapeake expansion has clear demand backing \u2192 **2/3**\n4. Capital allocation mix: balanced buyback + R&D (+ faster than sales [Actual]) + facility expansion + M&A priority [Actual] \u2192 **3/3**\n5. Guidance accuracy: Raised guidance in 3 consecutive quarters (FY25, Q1-26, Q2-26) [Actual]; consistent beat-and-raise \u2192 **3/3**\n**Total 13/15.** Insider ownership 0.37% \u2192 no ownership bonus; no insider transaction signal in pack \u2192 no adjustment.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\n**Step 2.5-1. R&D capitalization**\n- FY25 R&D not in pack; Q2-26 R&D $25M, growing faster than sales \u2192 FY26E R&D \u2248 $105M [Estimate]. Capitalized 3-yr straight-line: add back $105M R&D, deduct $35M amortization \u2192 adjusted OI \u2248 729 + 105 \u2212 35 = **$799M** (FY26E).\n- Adjusted operating margin \u2248 799/3,795 = 21.1% vs GAAP 19.2%.\n\n**Step 2.5-2. SBC**\n- FY25 SBC $22M [Filed]. Treat as cash expense. FCF adjusted for SBC: 554 \u2212 22 = **$532M** actual FY25. Forward DCF already deducts SBC.\n\n**Step 2.5-3. Operating leases**\n- FY25 operating leases $210M [Filed]. Present value ~$210M; adjusted net debt = 958 + 210 \u2212 371 = **$797M**. Net debt/EBITDA incl. leases \u2248 1.05x \u2014 still low.\n\n**Step 2.5-4. Brand/data asset**: not applicable \u2014 defense/nuclear component maker, brand value not central.\n\n**Step 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC**\n- GAAP NOPAT FY25: 634 \u00d7 (1\u22120.224) = $492M. Invested capital: equity 2,534 + debt 958 + op leases 210 = $3,702M \u2192 ROIC \u2248 **13.3%**.\n- Adjusted NOPAT (R&D capitalized): 492 + 105 \u2212 35\u00d70.776 \u2248 $570M; IC adj. + unamortized R&D (\u2248210) = $3,912M \u2192 adj ROIC \u2248 **14.6%**.\n- Gap 1.3pp \u2014 small; R&D not the dominant value-driver here.\n\n**Step 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett)**\nOwner Earnings = NI 484 + D&A 125 \u2212 maintenance capex (~70 [Assumption]) \u2212 \u0394WC (\u2212141 = cash release) \u2248 484 + 125 \u2212 70 + 141 = **$680M** on a normalized cash-release year. Normalizing \u0394WC to zero: \u2248 **$539M**. I use FCF net of SBC in modeling \u2014 \u2248 $532M actual FY25.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3. VALUATION (ENGINE 1 \u2014 DCF + MULTIPLES)\n\n## 3.1 Discount-rate derivation\n- Rf = 4.72% (10Y UST, Aug 21, 2026) [Actual \u2014 FRED]\n- ERP = 4.5% [Assumption]\n- Beta = 0.878 [Aggregator] \u2192 rounded to 0.90 [Assumption \u2014 pack beta is window-unspecified]\n- CoE = 4.72% + 0.90 \u00d7 4.5% = **8.77%**\n- Pre-tax cost of debt = 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 IG industrial, ~30bp over 10Y], tax rate 22.4% [Estimate \u2014 FY24 reported effective rate]\n- After-tax Kd = 3.88%\n- Weights: E = 23.451 / (23.451 + 0.958) = 96.1%; D = 3.9%\n- **WACC = 0.961 \u00d7 8.77% + 0.039 \u00d7 3.88% = 8.58% \u2192 use 8.6%**\n\n## 3.2 Explicit forecast\n| ($B) | FY25A | FY26E | FY27E | FY28E | FY29E | FY30E |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Revenue | 3.498 | 3.795 | 4.061 | 4.305 | 4.520 | 4.701 |\n| Growth | +12.1% | +8.5% | +7.0% | +6.0% | +5.0% | +4.0% |\n| EBIT margin | 18.1% | 19.2% | 19.5% | 19.8% | 20.0% | 20.0% |\n| EBIT | 0.634 | 0.729 | 0.792 | 0.852 | 0.904 | 0.940 |\n| NOPAT (tax 22.4%) | 0.492 | 0.566 | 0.615 | 0.662 | 0.703 | 0.731 |\n| D&A | 0.125 | 0.135 | 0.145 | 0.155 | 0.165 | 0.175 |\n| Capex | 0.090 | 0.117 | 0.135 | 0.145 | 0.155 | 0.165 |\n| \u0394WC (source = \u2212) | \u22120.141 | \u22120.020 | \u22120.015 | \u22120.010 | \u22120.005 | 0.000 |\n| SBC | 0.022 | 0.025 | 0.027 | 0.029 | 0.031 | 0.033 |\n| **FCF** | **0.554** | **0.595** | **0.613** | **0.653** | **0.687** | **0.708** |\n\n**Driver sourcing:**\n- FY26 revenue +8.5%: midpoint of management guidance 8\u20139% [Actual \u2014 Q2 2026 press release]\n- FY26 EBIT margin 19.2%: guidance 19.1\u201319.3% [Actual]\n- FY26 capex $117M: transcript says ~+30% vs FY25 $90M [Actual \u2014 management statement]; capex/sales ~3.1%\n- FY26 FCF $595M: guidance $585\u2013605M [Actual]\n- FY27 growth 7%: **[Assumption]** supported by backlog $4.5B (1.26x revenue) and book-to-bill 1.16\u20131.2x [Actual]; above long-run GDP, below the 10%+ upper end some bulls use\n- FY28\u201330 growth 5%\u21924%: **[Assumption]** fade toward nominal GDP; NOT established by management guidance \u2014 management's 2024 Investor Day three-year targets were referenced in the FY25 release but specific figures are **[Unconfirmed]**\n- Margin path to 20.0% ceiling: **[Assumption]** based on restructuring, operating leverage, and management's \"top quartile margin\" language [Actual]; 20% is my assumed plateau, not a stated target\n- \u0394WC source of cash through FY28: transcript states target **<18% of sales** [Actual \u2014 management]; after that \u0394WC neutral [Assumption]\n\n## 3.3 DCF output\nWACC 8.6%, terminal growth 3.0% [Assumption = long-run nominal GDP; not the pack's growth rate, and >3% is a red flag].\nDiscount factors: 0.9208, 0.8479, 0.7808, 0.7190, 0.6621\n- PV(2026\u20132030 FCF): 548 + 520 + 510 + 494 + 469 = **$2,541M**\n- Terminal value = 708 \u00d7 1.03 / (0.086 \u2212 0.03) = $13,022M; PV = $8,622M\n- EV = $11,163M\n- Net debt: LT debt